Step 0 supports a viable—but explicitly Track B—family. The paper’s native problem is an undirected graph with nonnegative integer edge weights and an unconstrained-cardinality clique whose induced-edge sum equals a target; it also explicitly notes that cardinality can be prescribed and that nonedges can be replaced by overweight edges, so complete weighted graphs remain native instances.

The paper proves compression lower bounds, not hardness of a random planted distribution. I therefore won’t use its \(O(n^{3-\varepsilon})\) kernel lower bound as a Track A claim. I’ll use fixed-cardinality exact-weight clique: inverse-generate a uniformly planted clique, with additive edge weights hiding per-vertex signed-radix potentials. A meet-in-the-middle \(k\)-sum algorithm exists and will be named, timed, and reported as the successful Track B reference algorithm; the compact route is to recover the potentials and decode the signed radix.
